WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:04.609
Hello everyone this video is about the

00:00:04.609 --> 00:00:06.500
greatest attacking player in chess history

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.690
He was known by many names the magician

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.210
from Riga, the pirate of latvia, Fortunes

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.910
favorite, the hypnotist and so on. This is

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:15.500
the moment you've all been waiting for.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.050
Top 10 facts about Mikhail Nekhemievich Tal

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.570
Number one - Mikhail Tal was born on 9th of

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.360
november 1936. He was a Soviet Latvian

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.980
chess grandmaster and the eighth world

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.489
champions from 1960 - 1961. Widely regarded

00:00:28.489 --> 00:00:30.380
as a creative genius and the best

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:31.880
attacking player of all time

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.559
Tal played in a daring combinatorial

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:35.690
style. His play was well known above all

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.429
for improvisation and unpredictability.

00:00:37.429 --> 00:00:39.859
Every game, he once said, was as inimitable

00:00:39.859 --> 00:00:42.710
and invaluable as a poem. The Mikhail Tal

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.269
memorial has been held in Moscow

00:00:44.269 --> 00:00:46.609
annually since 2006 to honor Tal's memory.

00:00:46.609 --> 00:00:49.339
Number to - Tal was born in Riga, Latvia

00:00:49.339 --> 00:00:51.440
into a jewish family. According to his

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.570
friend Gennadi Sosonko, his true father

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.699
was a family friend identified only as

00:00:55.699 --> 00:00:58.249
uncle Robert. From the very beginning of

00:00:58.249 --> 00:01:00.379
his life he suffered from ill health.

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:01.999
Tal learned to read at the age of three and

00:01:01.999 --> 00:01:03.890
was allowed to start university studies

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.439
while only 15. At the age of 8 Tal learned to

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:08.210
play chess while watching his father, a

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.280
doctor and a medical researcher.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.030
Number three

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.770
shortly thereafter he joined the Riga

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:14.899
palace for Young Pioneers chess club. His

00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:16.670
play was not exceptional at first but

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.890
you worked hard to improve. Alexander

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.049
Koblenz began tutoring Tal in 1949

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:23.270
after which Tal's game rapidly improved

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.000
and by 1951 he had qualified for the Latvian

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.189
championship. In the 1952 Latvian

00:01:28.189 --> 00:01:29.810
championship Tal finished ahead of his

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.329
trainer. Tal won his first Latvian title in

00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:34.700
1953 and was awarded the title of

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.920
candidate master. He became a Soviet

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.140
master in 1954 by defeating Vladimir

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.539
Saigin in a qualifying match. That same

00:01:41.539 --> 00:01:43.490
year he also scored his first win over a

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.590
grandmaster when Yuri Averbakh lost

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.509
on time in a drawn position. The opening

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:49.219
Tal used with white for this game was the

00:01:49.219 --> 00:01:51.710
Belgrade gambit.  Number 4 -  Tal won a  very

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.899
strong tournament at Zurich in 1959.

00:01:53.899 --> 00:01:55.969
Following the internalzonal the top players

00:01:55.969 --> 00:01:57.320
carried out to the candidates tournament,

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.979
Yugoslavia, 1959. Tal showed superior form by

00:02:00.979 --> 00:02:03.350
winning with 20 out of 28 points ahead of

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.060
Paul Kares with 18,5

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.399
followed by Tigran Petrosian, Vasily Smislov,

00:02:07.399 --> 00:02:09.020
the sixteen-year-old Bobby Fischer,

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.150
Svetozar Gligorić,  Friedrich Olafson and

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.970
Paul Benco. Tal's victory was

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:14.380
attributed to his dominance over the

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.000
lower half of the field while scoring

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.340
only one win and three losses vs Keres.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.900
He won all four individual games

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.670
against Fisher and took three and a half

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.860
points out of four from each Gligorić,

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.010
Olafson and Benko. Number five - In 1960

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.590
at the age of 23 Tal defeated the relatively

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.360
strategic Mikhail Botvinik in a world

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.580
championship match held in Moscow by 12

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.080
and a half to eight and a half points

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.600
six wins two losses and 13 draws making

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.310
him the youngest ever world champion

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.350
record later broken by Garry Kasparov

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.900
who earned the title at 22. Number six -

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.240
Botvninik, who had never faced Tal before the

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.070
title match, won the return match against

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:51.160
Tal in 1961 also held in moscow by 13

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.070
to 8. 10 wins to 5 with six draws. In

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:55.750
the period between the matches, Botvinik

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:57.760
had throughly analyzed Tal's style and

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.530
turned most of the return matches games

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.530
into slow wars of maneuver or end games

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.130
rather than the complicated tactical

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.050
melees which were Tal's happy hunting

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.090
ground. Tal's chronic kidney problems

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:08.620
contributed to his defeat and his

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.330
doctors in Riga advised that he should

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:11.709
postpone the match for health reasons.

00:03:11.709 --> 00:03:14.440
Yuri Averbakh claimed that Botvinik would agree to

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.260
a postponement only if Tal was certified unfit by

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.330
Moscow doctors. His short reign atop

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:20.920
the chess world made him one of the

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:23.140
so-called two winter Kings, who interrupted

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:26.400
Botvinik's long reign from 19 48-1963

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:26.980
the other

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.040
was Smislov, world champion from 1957 -1958.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:33.190
His highest ELO rating was 2705

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.769
achieved in 1980. His highest historical

00:03:35.769 --> 00:03:39.169
chest metric rating was 2799

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:39.840
Number seven

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.060
12th Round of 24th USSR Championship in 1957:

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.060
Mikhail Tal adjourned his game with Boleslavsky in a very difficult position.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.100
Straight after play, Tal went off to a date with a girl!

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:56.000
While the other players were enjoying their sleep, Tal spent the time walking around the streets of Moscow.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.540
Next to the Bielorussky railway station they crossed the road in the wrong place and the policeman arrested them and brought to the police station.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:08.700
The young lieutenant who was on duty turned to look at them with dissatisfaction and returned to the task he had had to break off ---

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:17.120
he was sitting with a chess board in front of him. Tal glanced at the board and couldn't suppress a smile. The police officer was analyzing Tal's adjourned game with Boleslavsky.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.600
Obviously the adjourned game had been dictated in the evening sports bulletin on the radio.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:29.800
Tal was unable to restrain himself and commented on a move made on the board. Instead of replying, the lieutenant pushed the board away from him and in a bored voice asked,

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:40.520
"Name?" "Tal." "What, another one?" "You'll laugh, I know," said Misha, "But I'm not another one, but the very man himself." A minute later, they were analyzing the position together.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:45.700
Tal went home only at 7 am. Despite the help of the police, the game was still lost on resumption.

00:04:46.379 --> 00:04:48.120
Number eight - here's an anegdote i read in a

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.620
book called Life and games of Mikhail Tal

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:56.620
Tal was playing against grandmaster Vasiukov. They reached a very complicated position and Tal was considering a knight sacrifice

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:02.680
(I know what you're thinking... What else would Tal be considering :) But, to his horror, he couldn't find any good continuations.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:08.560
Suddenly, he started to think about a Hippopotamus drowning in a swamp. He tried to figure out how to save the poor hypo.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:14.880
After 40 minutes of thinking about this problem, Tal realized that he couldn't save the poor hypo and that some things simply can't be calculated.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.160
He realized that the knight sacrifice was purely intuitive and it promised an interesting game.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:25.680
The following day, the newspapers wrote how Tal, after thinking for 40 minutes about the position, managed to calculate all the variations and

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.320
win the game with a great knight sacrifice. Little did they know that a poor hypo had to drown for Tal to win the game.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:32.120
Number nine

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:37.500
Mikhail Tal married three times. In 1959, he married 19-year-old Russian actress Salli Landau.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.140
This marriage lasted for 11 years and ended in divorce in 1970.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:47.460
His second marriage too did not last long.
He later on married Angelina Petukhov who worked in the Riga Shakhmaty magazine.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:54.140
He had been a sickly child though he had fairly good health during his youth. He suffered from kidney problems that intensified over time.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:57.920
He underwent several surgeries over the years and even lost a kidney to his illness.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:03.960
An eccentric man with a bohemian lifestyle, he became addicted to smoking and drinking which further affected his health.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:10.180
Mikhail Tal died on 28 June 1992 of a haemorrhage in the esophagus. He was just 55.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:13.060
And number ten - To understand Mikhail Tal a bit more,

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:18.200
here is what Garry Kasparov said about him: 
I worked a bit with Tal. Around 1980, he visited Baku,

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:24.440
we played a couple of training games, and the chess contact wasn't lost until Tal's very last days. There was a blitz tournament in Moscow,

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:31.500
one month before Tal's death. He looked horrible. But Tal was still Tal. In this blitz tournament, I lost my only game to him.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:37.160
I retaliated in the second round, but the fact was that until the very end, he still had this vision of games.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:41.140
He was the only one I knew who didn't calculate the lines, he saw them.

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:48.700
We calculate: he does this then I do that. And Tal, through all the thick layers of variants, saw that around the 8th move, it will be so and so.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:52.820
Some people can see the mathematical formulae, they can imagine the whole picture instantly.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:59.920
An ordinary man has to calculate, to think this through, but they just see it all. It occurs in great musicians, great scientists.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:07.600
Tal was absolutely unique. His playing style was of course unrepeatable. I calculated the variants quickly enough, but these Tal insights were unique.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:10.880
He was a man in whose presence others sensed their mediocrity.

